	In relational databases you should not care much about indices when=20
writing simple queries.  They are used automagically by the query=20
planner according to need.  Many times the planner will do a better job=20
of improving performance than a common programmer.  And if you decide to=20
change your indices, there's no need to rewrite your programs and =
queries.

	I would recommend that anyone coming to relational databases read "An=20
Introduction to Database Systems", 7th Ed, by Chris J Date -=20
Addison-Wesley, October 1.999, ISBN 0201385902; around US$ 58 used, US$=20
72 new.  The relational world is very, very different from "navigational=20
databases", and even if it is far simpler for the user it takes some=20
getting used to by programmers.



